        "heading": "Harbourline's Assistant Leaves a Slip for Every Prompt",
        "content": "**The setting.** Harbourline Insurance gave its claims department Microsoft 365 Copilot in September. Claims handlers use it to summarise claim files and draft letters. You are the analyst on shift in Harbourline's SOC (security operations centre), and your lead, Dana Whitlock, wants every analyst able to read the assistant's audit trail before the first real question arrives.\n\n**Two layers.** An assistant produces two kinds of evidence. The activity record says who used the assistant, when, from where, in which app and which resources it referenced. The conversation text, meaning the prompt and the answer, is kept elsewhere. The CopilotInteraction audit record holds message identifiers and resource references, and it does not hold the words of the prompt or of the answer. Think of the visitor book at a records room: it shows who came in and which cabinet was opened, not what they read or why.\n\n**Where the record is written.** Microsoft 365 writes Copilot activity to its unified audit log, the shared log of Microsoft 365 services. Each prompt and its answer produce a record whose Operation is CopilotInteraction and whose RecordType is 261.\n\n### The envelope: fields every Microsoft 365 audit record has\n\n| Field | What it tells you |\n|---|---|\n| Id | Unique identifier of the audit record |\n| CreationTime | When the record was generated, in UTC (Coordinated Universal Time) |\n| UserId | The UPN (user principal name), the sign-in name that looks like an email address |\n| UserKey | An alternative ID for the same user |\n| UserType | 0 is a regular user, 2 is an administrator |\n| ClientIP | IP address of the device that was used |\n| Workload | Copilot |\n\n### The AI block: CopilotEventData\n\n| Field | What it tells you |\n|---|---|\n| AppHost | The application that hosted the chat, for example BizChat, Word, Teams or Outlook |\n| Contexts | Where the user was, such as the document that was open |\n| ThreadId | The ID of the conversation thread: records with the same ThreadId belong to one conversation |\n| Messages | One entry per prompt or response, with an Id and an isPrompt flag |\n| AccessedResources | The files, documents and emails Copilot accessed to answer |\n\nBizChat is the Microsoft 365 Copilot Chat client. AppHost names an application, not a device.\n\n### Reading one AccessedResources entry\n\nEach entry has a Name (the readable file name), a SiteUrl (the URL or full path of the resource), a Type (such as docx or xlsx), an Action (commonly Read) and a Status (success or failure). If the resource carries a sensitivity label, a classification tag such as Confidential, the entry also has a SensitivityLabelId. An empty AccessedResources list is ordinary: Copilot answered without reading any file.\n\n**One caution about ClientIP.** Microsoft documents that for some services ClientIP might be the address of a trusted application calling the service on the user's behalf. Treat ClientIP as a lead, and confirm the location in the sign-in log of the identity provider, the system that checks who is signing in.\n\n**What to ask of every record.** Who (UserId), when (CreationTime in UTC), from where (ClientIP), in which app (AppHost), which conversation (ThreadId) and what was touched (AccessedResources).",
