        "type": "reading",
        "id": "aiua-r2",
        "heading": "A Second Vendor: The Claude Enterprise Activity Record",
        "content": "**A second assistant.** Harbourline's subrogation counsel, the lawyers who recover money from third parties, use Claude Enterprise. Its trail has a different shape, and reading both side by side is the fastest way to see what every AI audit trail has in common.\n\n**Where the record comes from.** Anthropic exposes the trail through its Compliance API. An API (application programming interface) is a way for one program to ask another for data. Harbourline's SIEM (security information and event management system, the platform that collects and searches logs) runs a collector, a small scheduled program, that reads the Activity Feed and stores each activity. Activities are queryable within 1 minute of occurring and are retained for 6 years.\n\n### The activity record\n\n| Field | What it tells you |\n|---|---|\n| id | Unique identifier for the activity |\n| created_at | When the activity occurred |\n| type | The activity type, for example claude_chat_created or claude_file_uploaded |\n| organization_id, organization_uuid | The organisation where it occurred |\n| actor | Who or what performed the activity |\n| claude_chat_id, claude_file_id, claude_project_id | The chat, file or project the activity is about |\n\n### The actor comes in several kinds\n\nThe field actor.type tells you which other actor fields exist.\n\n| actor.type | Meaning | Fields that come with it |\n|---|---|---|\n| user_actor | A signed-in user took the action | email_address, user_id, ip_address, user_agent |\n| api_actor | A request used a customer-issued API key | api_key_id, ip_address, user_agent |\n| unauthenticated_user_actor | An action before sign-in completed | unauthenticated_email_address, ip_address, user_agent |\n\nA user agent is the text a client program sends to describe itself, such as a browser name and version.\n\n**Which identifier to trust.** The user_id does not change when the user's email or display name changes, so Anthropic says to use user_id, not email_address, as the primary join key. A join key is a value you copy from one log to find the matching rows in another.\n\n### Four rules for reading this trail\n\n1. The Activity Feed does not capture the prompt text or model responses inside chats.\n2. A viewed activity, such as claude_chat_viewed, means a Claude app loaded content, not that a person viewed it. Repeated loads are not deduplicated.\n3. Activities about a file do not include its name: the filename field is always null, an empty string or omitted. To learn the name, pass the claude_file_id to the file metadata endpoint, which needs a Compliance Access Key with the read:compliance_user_data scope.\n4. Most calls to the Compliance API itself emit compliance_api_accessed activities, and their actor is an api_actor. The value in actor.api_key_id attributes the access to a specific key.\n\n**Rule 4 in practice.** Harbourline's own collector reads the feed every few minutes, so the feed holds a steady stream of compliance_api_accessed activities from the collector's key. They carry no email address because no person is involved.\n\n**Same idea, different names.** Copilot's UserId and UserKey correspond to Claude's actor.email_address and actor.user_id. Copilot's ThreadId corresponds to claude_chat_id. Copilot's AccessedResources corresponds to claude_file_id and claude_project_id. Neither trail holds the conversation text.",
