        "type": "reading",
        "id": "shai-r1",
        "heading": "Reading the Gateway Row: Who Sent What to Which AI Tool",
        "content": "**Where you are.** Northwick Logistics moves freight for retailers across the north of England. It has no written AI policy yet. IT has approved exactly one AI tool: Loadwise Assist, at assist.loadwise.example, in a company workspace for a pilot group in Customer Service. Everything else is unsanctioned. A sanctioned tool is one the organisation has approved; an unsanctioned tool is any other. Unsanctioned AI use for work is called shadow AI. Your job this week is to find it.\n\n**The log.** Northwick sends all web traffic through Zscaler Internet Access, a secure web gateway (SWG): a proxy that checks every web request and writes one log row per request. The rows reach the SIEM (security information and event management system, the central log store) as JSON.\n\n### The fields you will use\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| user, department | The person and their team |\n| devicehostname, ClientIP | The device and its internal address |\n| hostname, url | The site and the full address requested |\n| urlcategory | The category the gateway gave the site |\n| appname | The cloud application the gateway recognised |\n| requestmethod | GET fetches a page; POST sends data to the site |\n| requestsize | Bytes the device sent |\n| responsesize | Bytes the site sent back |\n| action, reason | Allowed or Blocked, and why |\n\nZscaler places generative AI sites in a URL category named AI and ML Applications. That category is your starting filter.\n\n### Reading one row\n\nRead every row in the same order: who, where, which direction, what the gateway did.\n\n1. **Who:** user and department. Finance, HR (human resources) and Legal handle the data that hurts most when it leaves.\n2. **Where:** hostname and urlcategory. Is the host the sanctioned tool or something else?\n3. **Which direction:** requestmethod and requestsize. Upload volume is the request size, not the response size. A typed prompt is small. A request of several megabytes is a file or a very long paste.\n4. **What the gateway did:** action. Allowed means the data left.\n\n### Three kinds of row\n\n- **A visit:** GET requests only, small requestsize. Someone looked at a page.\n- **Shadow use:** a POST with data to a host that is not the sanctioned tool. This is what you are hunting.\n- **Sanctioned use:** traffic to assist.loadwise.example from a member of the pilot group. A rule may still fire on it. When a rule catches exactly the behaviour it was written for and the activity is authorised, the verdict is benign true positive, not false positive. The rule worked; the activity was allowed.\n\n### What the row cannot tell you\n\nThe gateway does not know which account the person signed in with. A row to assist.loadwise.example looks the same for the company workspace and for a private account someone opened with a personal email address. The gateway also sees only traffic that passes through it: a laptop on home Wi-Fi without the Zscaler agent leaves no row at all.\n\n### What an analyst asks\n\n- Is the host on the approved list?\n- Is the user in the group the approval covers?\n- Did data go out (POST, large requestsize), or was it a visit?\n- Is it one person, or several people in one team?",
