        "type": "reading",
        "id": "aianu-r1",
        "heading": "Normal First: Baselines and Peers at Calder & Rowe",
        "content": "**The setting.** Calder & Rowe is a law firm of about 300 people. Every lawyer and paralegal has a seat in the firm's enterprise AI (artificial intelligence) assistant, Claude Enterprise, reached through single sign-on (SSO: one firm login that opens many applications). The assistant's audit feed, the Compliance API Activity Feed, flows into the firm's SIEM (security information and event management: the system that collects logs and raises alerts). You are the analyst watching it.\n\n**Anomalous is not malicious.** An anomaly is activity that differs from a baseline. A baseline is a summary of how an account behaved in the past. A paralegal who works late before a closing is anomalous and innocent. Your job is to explain the difference or escalate it.\n\n### Four questions\n\n| Question | You compare | Field in the Claude record |\n|---|---|---|\n| From where? | Network and client | actor.ip_address, actor.user_agent |\n| When? | Hour and weekday | created_at |\n| How much? | Count per day | number of records of one type |\n| What kind? | Action and target | type, claude_project_id, claude_file_id |\n\n### Count events that a person causes\n\nNot every record is a human action. A record whose type ends in _viewed, such as claude_chat_viewed, is written each time a Claude app loads the content from the servers. Repeated loads are not deduplicated, and the mobile, desktop and web apps load at different moments, sometimes in the background. For volume, count claude_chat_created and claude_file_uploaded, because a person causes those directly.\n\n### Compare with the person, then with the peers\n\nUse the median, the middle value when the days are sorted, because one extreme day barely moves it. Imogen Hartley, a paralegal in the Corporate group, has a median of 12 chats created per working day. A peer group is a set of accounts expected to behave alike, here the eleven Corporate paralegals. Their median today tells you whether the whole group moved. If the peers rose together, look for a deadline or a rollout. If only one account rose, look closer at that account.\n\nA simple measure is the ratio: today's count divided by the account's own median. A ratio near 1 is an ordinary day. The firm's rule raises an alert at a ratio of 4 or more.\n\n### Three verdicts for an alert\n\n- **False positive**: the rule misfired. The activity is not what the rule was built to catch.\n- **Benign true positive**: the rule caught exactly what it targets, and the activity is real but authorised.\n- **True positive**: the rule caught what it targets and nobody authorised it.\n\nA volume alert with a confirmed business reason, from the usual network and device, is a benign true positive. Calling it a false positive would invite someone to weaken a rule that worked.\n\n### What an analyst asks\n\n- Which event type did the rule count?\n- Is the count high for this person, for the peers, or for both?\n- Is there a business reason, and who can confirm it?",
