        "type": "reading",
        "id": "aianu-r2",
        "heading": "Who Is Really in the Session: Sign-in Context and the Search-Engine Pattern",
        "content": "**Two logs, one session.** The assistant sits behind the firm's identity provider (IdP: the system that checks who you are), Microsoft Entra ID. Entra's sign-in log records how a session began. The Claude activity feed records what the session then did. You need both.\n\n### Reading the sign-in row\n\n| Field | What it tells you |\n|---|---|\n| IPAddress | The address of the client that signed in |\n| AutonomousSystemNumber | The network operator that owns the address (ASN) |\n| Location | A two-letter country code |\n| DeviceDetail | Device ID, operating system and browser, and whether the device is managed and compliant |\n| ResultType | 0 means success, any other value is a failure |\n| IsInteractive | True when the user gave a password or an MFA response |\n| RiskEventTypes_V2 | Risk detections attached to this sign-in |\n\nMFA is multi-factor authentication, a second proof of identity such as a phone prompt. Two risk values matter today. The value unfamiliarFeatures means the sign-in had properties this user has not shown before, such as IP, ASN, location, device or browser. The value anonymizedIPAddress means an anonymous address, for example the Tor browser or an anonymous VPN (virtual private network). A firm laptop appears with DeviceDetail.isManaged and DeviceDetail.isCompliant set to true and a device ID. A machine the firm does not know appears with those fields empty or false.\n\n### Joining the two logs\n\nA Claude record by a signed-in user carries actor.user_id, actor.email_address, actor.ip_address and actor.user_agent. Anthropic recommends actor.user_id as the primary join key, because it does not change when the user's email or display name changes. Join actor.ip_address to network and VPN logs, and use created_at for the time window.\n\n### The search-engine pattern\n\nAn intruder with a stolen session does not need to browse folders. The assistant answers questions about firm documents in plain language, with the victim's permissions. The activity record holds no prompt text, so you look for the shape of the session:\n\n- a sign-in from a new network or device, then\n- many claude_chat_created records in a short time, then\n- claude_project_viewed records naming projects the account never opened, then\n- access failures such as claude_file_access_failed, and sometimes\n- claude_chat_deleted records soon after, to remove traces.\n\nViewed records are poor for counting activity, but they are good for breadth: count the distinct claude_project_id values that are new for this account.\n\n### Benign explanations to test first\n\n- Travel. Confirm by phone or through a manager, because the user's email or chat may be in the intruder's hands.\n- The firm's VPN or a new office line. Check the address against the firm's known ranges.\n- A new laptop. The device changes, but the network and the hours stay the same.\n\nOne deviation is a question. Several deviations at once, with no business reason, are a true positive: a new country, an unmanaged device, a burst of chats, projects from other practice groups and deletions do not happen together by accident.\n\n### What an analyst asks\n\n- Which layer is new: address, operator, country, device, or all of them?\n- Do the sign-in log and the activity feed agree about where the session is?\n- How many projects were touched that this account never touched before?",
