        "type": "reading",
        "id": "aianu-r3",
        "heading": "Rare and Powerful: Exports, Keys, Settings, and What a Rule Needs",
        "content": "**Some events need no baseline.** Chats happen all day, so you need statistics to spot the odd one. A few actions are so rare and so powerful that every occurrence is reviewed. For these the firm keeps a short list of people allowed to perform them and checks each occurrence against that list and against a change ticket, the written approval for a planned change.\n\n### Events with a standing rule at Calder & Rowe\n\nThe names are activity types from Anthropic's Compliance API reference.\n\n| Activity type | Why the firm reviews it |\n|---|---|\n| org_data_export_started, org_data_export_completed, org_data_export_accessed | A large amount of data leaves in one action |\n| api_key_created, admin_api_key_created | A key is used without a password or MFA prompt |\n| compliance_api_accessed | Someone read the firm's compliance data |\n| org_sso_toggled, org_ip_restriction_deleted | The sign-in rules or the network limits changed |\n| integration_user_connected | The assistant was connected to a new source |\n| claude_organization_settings_updated | A setting changed, including the data retention periods |\n\n### Read the actor first\n\nThe field actor.type says who or what acted. A user_actor is a signed-in person. An api_actor is a request made with an API key (API: application programming interface, the way programs talk to a service), and actor.api_key_id names the key. The firm's SIEM collector reads the feed with its own key, so compliance_api_accessed records from that key and address are expected. The same activity type from any other key is a finding.\n\n### The deletion trap\n\nA burst of claude_chat_deleted after a burst of new chats suggests clean-up. The opposite conclusion is unsafe. When the firm's retention policy removes a chat, the feed records no claude_chat_deleted at all, so a missing deletion record does not mean the content still exists.\n\n### What a behavioural rule needs\n\nWhether the firm buys a UEBA product (user and entity behavior analytics: software that profiles accounts) or writes its own queries, each rule needs the same parts: an entity to profile (actor.user_id), a signal to count (claude_chat_created), a baseline (window and median), a peer group, joined context (sign-in country, device), exclusions for known benign sources, and an output that shows the usual value next to the observed value. An alert that only says that something is unusual cannot be triaged.\n\n### The order of work on an alert\n\nThe firm's procedure for an anomalous-use alert has six steps, always in this order:\n\n1. Read which event type and which baseline the rule used.\n2. Compare the count with the account's own baseline and with the peer group.\n3. Pull the sign-in context: address, operator, country and device.\n4. Check breadth and clean-up: new projects, access failures, deletions.\n5. Test the benign explanations with someone who can confirm them, by phone.\n6. Decide the verdict and, for a true positive, escalate for containment.\n\nThe order protects you from two mistakes. Calling the user before you understand the rule wastes their time on a misfire. Containing an account before you test the benign explanations locks out a paralegal on the night before a closing.\n\n### What an analyst asks\n\n- Is the actor on the short list for this action, and is there a ticket?\n- Which key made this call, and is it the collector's?",
