        "heading": "A Key Is a Password That Spends Money",
        "content": "**The idea.** An API (application programming interface) is the way one program asks another for a service. An API key is a secret string the program sends with each request, so the service knows which account to serve and which account to bill. An LLM (large language model) is the kind of AI model behind chat assistants, and its providers charge per token, a small piece of text. Whoever holds the key spends the owner's money.\n\n**The story.** Quillfeather Games is a small studio. Its writers are testing an LLM that invents lines for NPCs (non-player characters, the figures in a game that the computer controls). The prototype calls Amazon Bedrock, the AWS service that hosts models, and signs each call with an AWS access key.\n\n### Two parts and a prefix\n\nAn AWS access key has an access key ID and a secret access key. The ID is written into audit logs, and the secret is not. The first four characters of the ID tell you what you are dealing with.\n\n| Prefix | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| AKIA | A long-term key of an IAM (Identity and Access Management) user |\n| ASIA | A temporary key issued by the AWS Security Token Service |\n\nAn ID that starts with AKIA is a long-term key that works until a person revokes it. An ID that starts with ASIA is temporary and expires by itself. One IAM user can hold at most two access keys.\n\n### Where keys leak\n\n- **Code repositories.** A developer commits a file that contains the key. Deleting the line in a later commit does not help, because the key stays in the history.\n- **Client-side code.** A key shipped inside a web page or a mobile app can be read by anyone who has the device.\n- **Logs and shared text.** Build logs, chat messages, tickets and wiki pages.\n\n### What GitHub records\n\nSecret scanning is the GitHub feature that searches the entire Git history on all branches for credentials. Each finding is an alert with a few fields worth knowing: state (open or resolved), validity (active, inactive or unknown) and publicly_leaked. A validity of active means the key still works, so treat the alert as an incident.\n\nThe GitHub audit log keeps the surrounding events. Each one has an action name:\n\n| Action | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| secret_scanning_alert.create | GitHub detected a secret and created an alert |\n| secret_scanning_alert.public_leak | An alert's secret was leaked in a public repository |\n| secret_scanning_push_protection.bypass | A user overrode the block that stops secrets being pushed |\n| repo.access | The visibility of a repository changed |\n\nA repo.access event carries visibility and previous_visibility. A repository that changes from private to public exposes every secret in its history at that moment.\n\n### What an analyst asks\n\n- Is the key long-term or temporary?\n- Is the alert's validity active?\n- Is the repository public, and since when?\n- Which identity owns the key, and what may it do?",
