        "heading": "The LLMjacking Pattern in CloudTrail",
        "content": "**The idea.** LLMjacking is the name researchers at Sysdig gave to using stolen cloud credentials to run someone else's hosted LLMs. The intruder resells the access, and the victim pays for the tokens. The pattern leaves a recognisable trail in AWS CloudTrail, the audit log of API calls in an AWS account.\n\n### What CloudTrail gives you\n\nBedrock records carry the eventSource bedrock.amazonaws.com. Model invocations are logged as management events, which CloudTrail records by default. CloudTrail records who called which model from where, but not the prompt or the response.\n\n### The stages and their event names\n\nThe grouping into stages is this room's own summary of the published cases.\n\n| Stage | Event name | What the caller learns or does |\n|---|---|---|\n| Enumerate | ListFoundationModels, GetFoundationModelAvailability | Which models exist and are available |\n| Check access | InvokeModel that fails with ValidationException | Whether the key may invoke, at no token cost |\n| Check logging | GetModelInvocationLoggingConfiguration | Whether prompts and responses are being logged |\n| Enable | PutFoundationModelEntitlement | Switches on a model the account had not enabled |\n| Invoke | InvokeModel, InvokeModelWithResponseStream, Converse, ConverseStream | Runs a model, and the stream variants send the answer back in pieces |\n\nIn order, the caller enumerates models, checks access, checks logging, enables models and invokes at volume, and the bill follows.\n\n### Why a failed call is good news for the intruder\n\nThe access check is an InvokeModel request with a parameter that no model accepts, in the first published case a token limit of minus one. A ValidationException on a deliberately invalid request shows the key is allowed to invoke the model, because a key without permission gets AccessDenied. No tokens are generated, so nothing shows up in usage or cost. The CloudTrail record is the only trace.\n\n### Two traps for detection rules\n\nConverse writes its own event name and produces no InvokeModel record, so a rule that matches only InvokeModel misses half of the invocation operations. And some intruders call DeleteModelInvocationLoggingConfiguration to switch prompt logging off: CloudTrail keeps recording the calls, but the prompts are gone.\n\n### The fields to read\n\n- userIdentity.accessKeyId: the key that signed the call.\n- sourceIPAddress: where the call came from.\n- userAgent: the client software. An application shows its SDK, and the published cases showed strings such as Python/3.11 aiohttp/3.9.5.\n- awsRegion: the Region that received the call.\n- errorCode and requestParameters.modelId: what failed, and which model was named.\n\nThe same access key ID appearing from an address it has never used before is the strongest single signal. To follow one caller through the log, search for the access key ID in userIdentity.accessKeyId.\n\n### What an analyst asks\n\n- Which stage is this event?\n- Has this key ever used this address, client or Region?\n- Did an invocation succeed after the checks?",
