        "heading": "One Email, One Gateway Record: Reading the Shield Verdict",
        "content": "**The setting.** Fenmoor Retail's support team uses an AI assistant, Fenmoor Assist, that reads each incoming customer email and drafts a reply for a human agent to review. The assistant is built on a large language model (LLM), a program that predicts text and follows instructions written in plain language. That strength is also the risk: an email can contain a sentence written for the assistant instead of for the agent. This is indirect prompt injection, which an earlier room explains. Here you learn to find it in the logs.\n\n### The path of an email\n\nEvery call the assistant makes passes through an API gateway. An API (application programming interface) is the way one program calls another, and a gateway is a checkpoint in front of a service that records each request. Fenmoor uses Azure API Management. Its records land in the SIEM (security information and event management system, the SOC's central log search) as rows of the table ApiManagementGatewayLogs. For each email the assistant makes two calls:\n\n1. A check call to Prompt Shields, the classifier in Azure AI Content Safety that looks for prompt injection. A classifier is a model trained to sort text into categories, here attack or no attack.\n2. A model call that produces the draft reply.\n\n### What the check call contains\n\nThe request has two fields. The field userPrompt holds what the user typed, at Fenmoor the agent's instruction, such as: draft a reply. The field documents holds third-party content, at Fenmoor the customer's email.\n\nThe response has two parts:\n\n| Response field | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| userPromptAnalysis.attackDetected | true or false for the text in userPrompt |\n| documentsAnalysis | a list with one attackDetected value for each document provided |\n\nA true value under documentsAnalysis points at third-party content, which means an indirect attempt. A true value under userPromptAnalysis points at the person typing, which means a direct attempt.\n\n### Where you read it\n\nFenmoor's gateway logs what the application sent and what the classifier sent back. In the gateway row these columns matter most:\n\n| Column | What it tells you |\n|---|---|\n| OperationId | Which operation was called, for example shieldPrompt |\n| ApimSubscriptionId | Which application's subscription made the call |\n| CallerIpAddress | The address that called the gateway. This is the application server, not the customer |\n| ResponseCode | Whether the call itself succeeded. 200 means the check ran |\n| BackendRequestBody | What the application sent: the userPrompt and the documents |\n| BackendResponseBody | What the classifier answered: the verdict |\n| CorrelationId | The unique identifier of this one request |\n\n### Two limits to remember\n\nFirst, the answer is only true or false. It does not name the sentence that triggered it, so you read the content yourself in BackendRequestBody. Second, the Prompt Shields API does not block anything by itself. The application decides what to do with the result. Fenmoor Assist is written to send a flagged email to a manual queue and skip the model call.\n\n### What an analyst asks\n\n- Which field was flagged: the user prompt or a document?\n- Which application made the call?\n- What did the application do next?\n\nA verdict proves that attack-like text arrived. It does not prove that the model saw it or obeyed it.",
