        "id": "fcall-r2",
        "heading": "What Abnormal Looks Like in Tool Calls",
        "content": "**The idea.** A tool name alone rarely tells you anything. Ledgerline calls send_statement_email hundreds of times a week. Abnormal shows up in four places: how often, with what values, in what order, and with whose approval.\n\n### 1. Baselines per tool\n\nA baseline is a description of normal built from past records. Build one per tool, not one for the whole assistant, because fifty calls a day is quiet for lookup_supplier and extraordinary for create_payment_batch. Larkspur's baseline for the last four weeks:\n\n| Tool | Typical per user per day | Typical argument values |\n|---|---|---|\n| lookup_supplier | 20 to 60 | One supplier id per call |\n| read_finance_document | 5 to 15 | Paths under /sites/Finance/AP |\n| send_statement_email | 3 to 10 | Recipient domains of onboarded suppliers |\n| create_payment_batch | 0 to 2 | Up to 40 payments per batch, known beneficiaries |\n\n### 2. Argument anomalies\n\nAn argument anomaly is a call to an allowed tool with a value outside what the tool is for. Check four families: the recipient (a domain never seen for this tool), the path (a folder outside the user's team, such as /sites/Finance/Payroll for an AP clerk), the query (no filter, or every row), and the amount (above the limit, or a beneficiary added the same day). A value in the right format is not automatically a right value: a schema only checks shape.\n\n### 3. Dangerous sequences\n\nSome calls are harmless alone and dangerous in order. The pattern to know by heart is a read of sensitive data followed by a write to a destination outside the organisation, inside the same conversation. MITRE ATLAS, the MITRE knowledge base of attacks on AI systems, names the sending half AML.T0086, Exfiltration via AI Agent Tool Invocation. A read followed by a send is also how the product is meant to work, so the rule needs one more condition: the destination is external or new.\n\n### 4. Missing human approval\n\nHuman approval means a person confirms a specific action before the application executes it. At Larkspur the approval lives in the payment workflow system. Four things can be wrong with it: there is no approval, the approval time is later than the execution time, the approver is the same person as the requester, or the approved values differ from the executed arguments.\n\n### Reading it together\n\nOne signal is a question. Several signals in one conversation are a case. A clerk who reads a payroll file, then sends an email to a new external domain, with no approval record, has hit three of the four places at once.\n\n### What is not an anomaly\n\nVolume rises for the whole team at month-end. A new supplier's domain appears on the day the supplier is onboarded. Both are explained by a business record outside the AI system, and that is the analyst's test: which record says this value was expected?",
