        "id": "aidat-r3",
        "heading": "Closing the Gap: Access Controls, Discovery Controls and Home-Built Assistants",
        "content": "**Finding is half the job.** The exposure ends only when the permission changes. That work belongs to the content owner and the SharePoint administrator, but you need to know which control does what, because some controls only hide content.\n\n### Access or discovery\n\n| Control | What it changes |\n|---|---|\n| Fix the permission | Access. The owner removes the organisation-wide group. This is the root fix |\n| Restricted access control | Access. Users not in the specified groups cannot reach the site or its content, even if they had prior permissions or a shared link |\n| Restricted Content Discovery | Discovery only. Content from the site stops appearing in organisation-wide search and Copilot responses. It does not change permissions |\n| DLP policy for the Copilot location | Assistant processing. Copilot does not process the content of items that carry a chosen sensitivity label |\n\nDLP stands for data loss prevention. Two details matter for your alerts. With that DLP policy, an excluded item can still appear in the citations of an answer although its content was not used. And Restricted Content Discovery is designed as a temporary control: users who already have access can still open the content directly, so it buys a pause for a review and nothing more.\n\n### The order of a case\n\nWork in this order, and do not skip the last step.\n\n1. Read the AccessedResources lists and note the sites and labels.\n2. Decide who is behind the account: origin, time, breadth and pace.\n3. Ask the SharePoint administrator and the site owner whether the user should have access.\n4. Limit discovery on the site while the permissions are reviewed.\n5. The owner fixes the permission.\n6. Re-run the hunt to confirm the reads have stopped, and look back for other users who reached the same site.\n\n### Assistants the council builds itself\n\nMirefield's digital team is also building a chatbot over policy documents. It uses retrieval-augmented generation (RAG): the application searches an index of documents and gives the best matches to the LLM. Here no vendor enforces permissions. If the application queries the index with its own service identity and adds no filter for the person asking, every user gets the reading rights of the application.\n\nThe defence is document-level access control: each indexed document carries who may read it, and every query is filtered by the identity of the person asking. In the security filter pattern the application adds a filter listing the caller's groups, and it must apply that filter to every query. Ask the developers for a retrieval log that records, for each query, the user, the filter that was applied and the identifiers of the documents returned. A retrieval with an empty filter is the clearest sign of a broken control.\n\n### What an analyst asks\n\nDid the fix change access, or only discovery? Has the hunt been re-run? For a home-built assistant: was a filter present on every query?",
        "checkpoint": {
          "question": "What does Restricted Content Discovery leave unchanged?",
          "options": [
            "Whether the site appears in organisation-wide search results",
            "Whether Copilot surfaces the site's content in its responses",
            "The site's permissions: people with access can still open files",
            "The audit records that were written before it was switched on"
          ],
          "answer": 2,
          "explanation": "Restricted Content Discovery changes discovery only. It does not change permissions, so users who already have access can still open the content directly. Appearing in organisation-wide search and in Copilot responses are exactly the two things it does change. The reading says nothing about it touching earlier audit records, and it is the permission statement that matters for your alerts."
        }
