        "id": "ailk-r2",
        "heading": "Endpoint, Browser and Network: Three Views and One Wrong Match",
        "content": "**Where the control stands decides what it sees.** Picture a letter leaving an office. One guard stands at the writer's desk and sees the page before it is sealed. A second sits inside one particular mail machine. A third stands at the exit and must open envelopes to read them.\n\n### Endpoint DLP: at the desk\n\nIt runs on an onboarded device and evaluates a paste or an upload on the device itself. In Chrome and Firefox it needs the Microsoft Purview extension. If data is never saved to a file on the local device, Endpoint DLP cannot scan or classify it.\n\n### Browser DLP: inside one machine\n\nPurview can inspect inside Microsoft Edge for Business without onboarding the device. For AI apps the organization does not manage it checks uploaded text and uploaded files, evaluating up to 4 MB of text and files up to 3 MB.\n\n### Network DLP: at the exit\n\nA secure web gateway (SWG) is a proxy that web traffic passes through. Tessaly uses Zscaler Internet Access. Each web transaction becomes one log row. The fields that matter for a leak:\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| user, department, devicehostname | Who authenticated to the gateway, and the machine |\n| hostname, appname, urlcategory | The site, the cloud application and the category the gateway assigned |\n| requestmethod | The HTTP method; prompts and uploads travel in POST requests |\n| requestsize | Bytes sent from the client to the server, the upload |\n| responsesize | Bytes the server returned |\n| dlpdictionaries | The DLP dictionaries, sets of patterns such as Credit Cards, that matched |\n| ssldecrypted | Whether the encrypted session was opened for inspection |\n| action | What the policy did, Allowed or Blocked |\n\nThe gateway inspects content only when ssldecrypted is Yes. It can see browsers and other applications alike. It records the user who authenticated to the gateway, never the account signed in on the AI site.\n\n### Which source answers which question\n\n- The endpoint record gives the sensitive information type and the unique count.\n- The gateway row gives the bytes that went up and whether the transaction was allowed.\n- The evidence copy, a duplicate of the matched item that DLP can store in an Azure storage account, gives the exact content.\n- The Justification gives the user's own stated reason.\n\n### When the match is wrong\n\nA dictionary matches patterns, not meaning. A false positive is a match on content that is not what the rule exists to protect. The classic case is sample data: a test file full of card-shaped numbers trips a Credit Cards dictionary although no cardholder exists.\n\nUse false positive only when the content is not the real thing. If real sensitive data went to an approved destination under an approved workflow, the rule was right and the activity was authorized: that is a benign true positive.\n\nTo test for sample data, look at the content, not the alert. Sequential or repeated values, names such as Test User, and a fixture file name are signs. The data owner, the person responsible for the dataset, confirms whether records are live or sample data, and the confirmation is written into the case.",
