        "id": "aiuc-r2",
        "heading": "Inventory, Priority and the Coverage Matrix",
        "content": "**Why the inventory comes first.** Kasia cannot rank threats to systems nobody has listed. She builds an AI asset inventory: one row per system with its owner, the identity that calls the model, the data it can reach, the actions it can take, who can reach it and the telemetry that exists today.\n\n### Ranking the rows\n\nNo standard gives a formula. Vantor's SOC uses a simple convention of its own: score three things from 1 to 3 and add them.\n\n- Exposure: 1 named internal users, 2 all staff or partners, 3 the public.\n- Data reach: 1 public data, 2 internal data, 3 regulated or secret data.\n- Agency: 1 answers only, 2 reads internal sources, 3 calls tools that change something.\n\nOne rule is applied before the score: a use case can be built now only if its data source is collected now. A high score with no telemetry produces a logging request first.\n\n### What is logged by default\n\nA coverage matrix has AI surfaces as rows and data sources as columns. To fill it Kasia needs the documented defaults.\n\n**Amazon Bedrock in CloudTrail.** Management events record control-plane operations and are logged by default. Data events are high-volume operations that CloudTrail does not log by default. Bedrock logs InvokeModel, InvokeModelWithResponseStream, Converse and ConverseStream as management events. InvokeAgent, Retrieve and RetrieveAndGenerate are data events: they appear only after advanced event selectors are configured for resource types such as AWS::Bedrock::AgentAlias and AWS::Bedrock::KnowledgeBase.\n\n**Bedrock model invocation logging.** A separate feature that collects the request data, response data and metadata of model calls. Model invocation logging is disabled by default. CloudTrail tells you who called which model. Only invocation logging holds what was said.\n\n**Microsoft 365 Copilot.** A CopilotInteraction audit record is generated automatically. It names the user, the time and the host application in AppHost, and it lists the files Copilot used in AccessedResources. Each resource entry can carry a SensitivityLabelId, the identifier of the sensitivity label on that file. The Messages property holds message identifiers and an isPrompt flag. The record has no property for the prompt text.\n\n### Vantor's matrix\n\n| Surface | Who called, and what | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| GridAssist, direct model call | Yes: CloudTrail management events | No: invocation logging is disabled |\n| FieldDesk, agent run | No: InvokeAgent data events are off | No |\n| Microsoft 365 Copilot | Yes: CopilotInteraction | Partial: resources listed, no prompt text |\n\nA cell marked No is written together with the action that would fix it and the owner who can take that action.\n\n### Reading a label identifier\n\nSensitivityLabelId is a GUID (globally unique identifier), not a name. To judge an alert the analyst needs enrichment: a lookup table that turns the identifier into a label name such as Restricted. Enrichment is a field on the use-case card for exactly this reason.\n\n### What an analyst asks\n\n- For this surface, which record names the caller and which holds the content?\n- Is that record on by default?\n- Which empty cell blocks the highest-priority use case?",
