        "type": "reading",
        "id": "aiuc-r3",
        "heading": "The Card, the Lifecycle and the First Alerts",
        "content": "**The card.** Kasia writes one card per behaviour. Vantor's card has these fields: objective, threat, reason, data sources, logic, enrichment, tuning, blind spots, validation, response and metrics. The layout borrows from the Alerting and Detection Strategy (ADS) framework published by Palantir, whose sections include Goal, Categorization, Blind Spots and Assumptions, False Positives, Validation, Priority and Response.\n\n### The lifecycle for one AI threat\n\nFor the threat 'a stolen key calls GridAssist's model', the steps run in this order:\n\n1. Pick the threat and read its ATLAS entry (AML.T0012).\n2. Find the inventory row for the system and its caller identity.\n3. Check the coverage matrix: is the data source collected?\n4. Write the logic in words, with field names.\n5. Validate: make it fire once on purpose.\n6. Deploy, then review the metrics.\n\nThe inventory comes before the logic because the row supplies the allow-list: GridAssist calls one model through one role. Validation comes before deployment because ADS defines it as the steps required to generate a representative true positive event which triggers the alert. ADS adds that an alert that cannot be made to fire may need to be refactored or discarded.\n\n### Record details that break logic\n\n**Streaming calls write two events.** For ConverseStream and InvokeModelWithResponseStream, Bedrock records the API call and then a stream-completion event when the response has finished. The completion event has eventType AwsServiceEvent and carries the token counts. It has the same eventName, eventSource, userIdentity, sourceIPAddress and userAgent as the call, so eventType is the only field that tells them apart. A use case that counts invocations must keep only records where eventType is AwsApiCall. The field serviceEventDetails.parentRequestId holds the requestID of the call that produced the completion event.\n\n**The logging itself can be switched off.** Model invocation logging is controlled by three operations: PutModelInvocationLoggingConfiguration, GetModelInvocationLoggingConfiguration and DeleteModelInvocationLoggingConfiguration. A delete removes the only source of prompt content, so Vantor has a card for it. In CloudTrail the identity block shows who did it: userIdentity.type, userIdentity.arn and, for an IAM user, userIdentity.userName. IAM is Identity and Access Management, the AWS service that defines users, roles and keys.\n\n### Three verdicts on a new use case\n\n- **True positive**: the rule caught its behaviour and nobody authorised it.\n- **Benign true positive**: the rule caught its behaviour and the activity was authorised, for example a validation test or a ticketed change. The rule is working, so it is not weakened.\n- **False positive**: the rule misfired on something it was not built for. The fix is in the logic.\n\nTo tell the first two apart the analyst checks the change calendar and asks the owner named in the inventory row. An action by an identity that never performs that operation, from an address outside the known ranges, with no change record, is treated as real.\n\n### Metrics\n\nEach card is reviewed on alerts per week, the share confirmed, the share closed as false positive and the days since its last successful validation.\n\n### What an analyst asks\n\n- Did this rule fire on the behaviour it was written for?\n- Was the activity authorised, and by whom?\n- If it misfired, which field would have separated the two cases?",
        "checkpoint": {
          "question": "Which field tells a streaming API call apart from its stream-completion event?",
          "options": [
            "eventName",
            "eventType",
            "userAgent",
            "eventSource"
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explanation": "The reading says the completion event has the same eventName, eventSource, userIdentity, sourceIPAddress and userAgent as the call, so eventType is the only field that tells them apart: AwsApiCall for the call, AwsServiceEvent for the completion."
        }
