        "heading": "One Record, One Rule: The Single-Event Match",
        "content": "**The job.** Pellham University has started using Amazon Bedrock, the AWS service that lets applications call large language models (LLMs, the text-generating models behind chat assistants). The security operations centre (SOC) has no rules for it yet. Ngozi Adeyemi, the detection engineer, asks you to help write the first ones. A detection rule is a saved query that a SIEM (security information and event management system, the platform that collects and searches logs) runs on a schedule.\n\n### The log\n\nBedrock activity is recorded by AWS CloudTrail, the AWS audit log. Each record is one API call (API means application programming interface, the way one program asks another to do something). These are the fields a rule can rely on:\n\n| Field | What it holds |\n|---|---|\n| eventSource | The service that was called. For Bedrock the value is bedrock.amazonaws.com |\n| eventName | The action, for example InvokeModel |\n| userIdentity.arn | Who called. An ARN (Amazon Resource Name) is the unique address of an identity |\n| userIdentity.accessKeyId | The access key ID that was used to sign the request |\n| sourceIPAddress, userAgent | The address and the client software the call came from |\n| eventType | AwsApiCall for a real API call |\n\nPrompts and answers are kept by a separate feature called model invocation logging, which is disabled by default. Pellham switched it on. Its configuration is removed with the call DeleteModelInvocationLoggingConfiguration and replaced with PutModelInvocationLoggingConfiguration. Someone misusing the models gains from either: the first stops the record of prompts, and the second can redirect it.\n\n### The rule\n\nOne such record is enough to alert on. This is a single-event match, the simplest of the three rule shapes. Pellham writes it in Sigma, a vendor-neutral rule format in YAML (plain text made of keys and indented values) that a converter turns into the query language of any SIEM.\n\nA Sigma detection has named blocks and a condition:\n\n- A block written as a map of field and value pairs matches only when all of its fields match. The elements of a map are joined with AND.\n- A field that is given a list of values matches when any one of them matches. List items are joined with OR.\n- Sigma compares values without regard to upper or lower case.\n- A second block can describe accepted activity. The condition selection and not filter_pipeline keeps every match except those.\n\nThe level field says how urgent an alert is, from informational to critical. The specification describes high as an event that should trigger an internal alert and requires a prompt review. Pellham sets this rule to high, because the action is rare and it removes evidence.\n\n### The exclusion\n\nThe university's deployment pipeline sets the logging configuration from code, using the role pel-iac-deploy. The filter names that role and only the Put call. It does not exclude the Delete call for anyone, and it does not exclude the role's other actions. An exclusion should be as narrow as the accepted activity, or it becomes a place to hide.\n\n### What you ask when it fires\n\nWho made the call, with which access key, from which address and client? Is there a change ticket or a pipeline run at that time?",
