        "type": "reading",
        "id": "airule-r2",
        "heading": "Counting: Thresholds, Baselines and the Double Count",
        "content": "**When one record is not enough.** Calling a model is what Pellham's applications are for, so no single InvokeModel record is suspicious. A stolen access key used to run a stranger's workload shows up as volume. For that you need the second rule shape, a threshold. It counts records per entity over a window and fires when the count passes a number.\n\nPellham counts model invocations (InvokeModel, InvokeModelWithResponseStream, Converse, ConverseStream) per calling identity per hour.\n\n### The query\n\nPellham's SIEM is Microsoft Sentinel, and its query language is KQL (Kusto Query Language). CloudTrail records land in a table named AWSCloudTrail, where the fields have been renamed: eventSource is EventSource, eventName is EventName, eventType is EventTypeName and userIdentity.arn is UserIdentityArn. In KQL the summarize operator groups rows that share the same values and computes a figure for each group. The function count() counts the rows in a group, and bin(TimeGenerated, 1h) rounds every timestamp down to the start of its hour, so each identity gets one row per hour.\n\n### The double count\n\nA streaming call (one that returns the answer piece by piece) writes two CloudTrail records. The first has the eventType AwsApiCall. The second is written when the stream finishes and has the eventType AwsServiceEvent. It is not an API call. A rule that counts model invocations must keep only AwsApiCall, or every streamed call is counted twice and the rule fires at half the real volume.\n\n### The threshold comes from a baseline\n\nA baseline is a measurement of normal, taken from your own history with the same grouping and window as the rule. Ngozi measured 21 days of hourly counts per identity:\n\n| Identity | 99th percentile per hour | Highest hour |\n|---|---|---|\n| pel-helpdesk-bot | 240 | 290 |\n| pel-research-llm | 90 | 130 |\n| pel-library-search | 35 | 50 |\n\nThe 99th percentile is the value that 99 percent of the hours stay at or below. A threshold belongs above the highest normal hour, with a margin, and low enough to catch abuse. Pellham chose more than 400 calls in an hour. That is above every highest hour in the table, so it would have raised no alert in those 21 days, and a hijacked key running thousands of calls would cross it at once. Its weakness: pel-library-search could grow to six times its normal volume without reaching it.\n\nIn Sentinel the alert threshold applies separately to each run of the rule. A rule that counts per hour needs a lookback of at least one hour.\n\n### The same shape for people\n\nStaff reach public AI sites through the university's web proxy, Zscaler Internet Access. Its web log has a user, a urlcategory, an action and a requestsize, the number of bytes the browser sent. A second threshold rule sums requestsize per user for the category AI and ML Applications and fires above 20 megabytes in a day.\n\n### Fired does not mean hostile\n\nA threshold rule that fires on real, authorised activity has worked correctly. That alert is a benign true positive. A false positive is different: the rule misfired, because the activity was not what the rule describes. The fix for the first is a narrow exclusion, and the fix for the second is to repair the rule.",
        "checkpoint": {
          "question": "Why must a rule that counts model invocations keep only records whose eventType is AwsApiCall?",
          "options": [
            "Because AwsServiceEvent records arrive a day late and would shift the hourly window",
            "Because AwsApiCall records are the only ones that carry the identity of the caller",
            "Because a streamed call also writes an AwsServiceEvent record, doubling the count",
            "Because AwsServiceEvent records exist only when data event logging is switched on"
          ],
          "answer": 2,
          "explanation": "A streaming call writes two records: an AwsApiCall record, and a second one with the eventType AwsServiceEvent when the stream finishes. Counting both doubles the figure for every streamed call. The reading says nothing about a day's delay. The second record is not described as lacking the caller. It is described as not being an API call, and it does not depend on data event logging."
        }
